{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to CyberGhost VPN for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4320,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Did you know that many of us have up to \n4% of our DNA from neanderthals? And that 100% of us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12960,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may have DNA from outer space? No joke. At least \nthe biochemistry that defined the coding system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20760,
    "dDurationMs": 6746,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of your DNA may have happened off-world, \nand perhaps even long before Earth existed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33920,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, just wanted to \nlet you know we have some new merch at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37320,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the merch store celebrating 10 years of \nSpace Time. We’ve got a limited edition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 year anniversary design as well as some \nclassic logo merch. Having his gear doesn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49680,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just make you incredibly cool, you also \nhelps us keep going for another decade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life is the coolest thing to have happened in \nour universe. It would be nice to know if it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61240,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happened anywhere else besides Earth—if nothing \nelse to know just how badly we’re screwing up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67440,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we flirt with self-extinction. But \nnot only have we never found credible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72680,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence of life on or from other worlds, we \nonly have the sketchiest of ideas of how it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78160,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "began on Earth. Makes it pretty difficult to say \nmuch about what’s happening out there. But one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encouraging detail is just how quickly life got \nstarted on Earth. The earliest fossils are dated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90120,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to within a few hundred million years of the Earth \nfirst cooling from its early molten hell-ball  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phase. And if you believe some of the more \ntentative biosignatures, it could be much earlier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, life got started so quickly on Earth \nthat some have argued that natural selection  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109680,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just didn’t have the time to take raw elements \nall the way to the first single-celled lifeform.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This difficulty led some scientists to propose \npanspermia— the idea that life did not start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122560,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Earth at all, but rather traveled here in \nthe form of extremely simple and presumably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremely robust simple organisms. It’s a \nfun story, and we’ve talked about it before,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it’s not supported by evidence and \ngenerally not considered particularly likely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is a middle road. Perhaps life didn’t \nstart in space, but maybe its building blocks did.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pseudo-panspermia is the idea that many of the \ncomplex molecules critical for abiogenesis—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation of life—were not formed on Earth, but \nrather in the depths of space, in some cases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long before the formation of itself. If these \nrained down on our planet in its early years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would allow our molecular ancestors to \nskip many steps in their path to the first cell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The evidence for pseudo-panspermia is rather \nstrong. And the most compelling of that evidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is just in, with the safe return of the OSIRIS-REx \nmission. But before we get to those results,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187160,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let's review what little we know \nabout the first formation of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life as we know it is based on a complex interplay \nof organic molecules, primarily nucleic acids,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proteins, lipids, and carbohydrates. In \nyour body, these work together to form  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the intricate machinery of the modern eukaryotic \ncell. Of course, the first life was much simpler,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209160,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and life’s precursor simpler still—for example, \nself-replicating RNA molecules of the RNA-world  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothesis. But how did a bunch of non-living \nchemicals even get to something as complex as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223360,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "RNA? Extremely broadly, assume that in some \nenergy and chemical-rich primordial soup,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230520,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where simple elements combined into \norganic molecules, when then combined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and recombined in a chain of trial and \nerror until this natural laboratory first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stumbled on self-catalyzing and \nthen self-replicating molecule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The step from organic molecules to complex, \nself-replicating RNA is still pretty mysterious,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252440,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if there have been recent advances. But \nthe pre-RNA part of the story? We’ve know that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible for decades. Back in 1952, a pair of \nscientists attempted to replicate the conditions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the early Earth, with liquid water “ocean” \nevaporating into an atmosphere of hydrogen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "methane and ammonia. An electrical spark \nsimulated primordial lightning powering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277360,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a chain or reactions that ultimately \nled to a variety of organic compounds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including five amino acids—the building \nblocks of proteins. This was the famous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Miller-Urey experiment, and it was our first \nhint at how the seeds of life may have formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293480,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That experiment has now been repeated in \na variety of ways, and we now know that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297520,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amino acids can spontaneously form given the \nright raw materials, an aqueous environment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303320,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and an energy gradient. The apparent ease with \nwhich amino acids form led scientists to wonder  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310720,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just how ubiquitous these organic molecules \nmight be, not just on Earth, but through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe. For example, in the chunks of rock \nand ice that rained down on the early Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321600,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first building our planet and then perhaps \nseeding it with the precursors to life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327400,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the first pieces of evidence that space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocks are packed with organic compounds \nwas the Murchison meteorite, which fell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334960,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Australia in 1969. This is a carbonaceous \nchondrite meteorite, so, made mostly of carbon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clay minerals, and water. But it was also \nfound to contain over 90 different amino acids,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including some that are not commonly found \non Earth, as well as the nucleobases purine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pyrimidine which are critical components \nof DNA and RNA, and other organic molecules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359680,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Carbon dating estimates that Murchison \nis around 7 billion years old,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so a couple of billion years older than \neven the Sun, meaning that complex organic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chemistry would have been occurring in \nspace long before life emerged on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374400,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s hard to be 100% certain about the origin of \nthe molecules found in the Murchison meteorite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380680,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the rock was in contact with Earth, which \nwe already know is infested with life. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387280,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the amino acids in the meteorite had one \ndistinct difference to those typically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found on Earth—they came in both left- and \nright-handed varieties—both chiralities, both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397960,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mirror reflections. But all amino acids produced \nby Earth life are left-handed—a chirality that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406080,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been locked in since the first common DNA or \nRNA ancestor of all life won that great molecular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "war 3.5 to 4 billion years ago. The presence \nof both chiralities in the Murchison meteor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us that these amino acids had an abiotic \norigin—supporting their pre-life formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426040,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pretty convincing, but the best \nway to be sure there’s no cross  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429460,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contamination from Earthly life is \nto get samples directly from space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there have been some incredible efforts to do \nthis. NASA’s stardust mission collected samples  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the coma of comet Wild 2 and returned them \nto Earth. The Japan Space Agency was the first to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land on an asteroid and return samples—twice, \nactually with Hayabusa and Hayabusa2. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ESA’s Rosetta mission was the first to send a \nlander to a comet where it analyzed samples on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460800,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "location. These and other missions have shown that \norganic compounds are abundant on space rocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468120,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how far along can this pre-biotic biochemistry \nreally advance in space? How much of a head start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475440,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might pseudo-panspermia have given to life on \nEarth? Some of that has now been answered by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482040,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA’s OSIRIS-REx mission. In our long tradition \nof unwieldy science acronyms, this stands for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Origins, Spectral Interpretation, Resource \nIdentification, and Security-Regolith Explorer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496400,
    "dDurationMs": 6141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spacecraft launched in 2016, with its sights\non an asteroid by the name of Bennu. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502541,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bennu is a pretty ordinary \ncarbonaceous asteroid of middling size,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507360,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at around 500 m in diameter. It’s part of the \nApollo group—a collection of more than 20,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513400,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroids with orbits that cross Earth’s. \nThat means they do sometimes slam into our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519680,
    "dDurationMs": 10280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet—like the one that exploded over Chelyabinsk \nin 2013. Or as in 2024 YR4, which has a miniscule  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance of hitting Earth in 2032. Bennu has \nno immediate chance of hitting us, but it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536200,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does make a close approach to Earth of around \n300,000 km or 24 Earth diameters every 6 years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The upside of these potentially cataclysmic \nneighbors is that they’re quite easy to visit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which OSIRIS-REx did. It started that journey \nin orbiting around the Earth, followed by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mad dash to catch up to and orbit the asteroid. \nThen after a few years of observation and analysis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a suitable landing site was identified and the \nspacecraft touched down. Samples were collected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568520,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before an easy escape from Bennu’s frankly \npathetic excuse for a gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575280,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OSIRIS-REx rejoined Earth’s solar orbit \nand dropped its sample capsule into our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580280,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere in September of 2023. And after \nall of that, the capsule’s parachute failed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586480,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its first parachute failed. The second opened \njust fine and the capsule landed right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on target in Utah. Since then, scientists \nhave been hard at work analyzing the sample,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the first results of the findings were \nfinally published in January this year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what did we find? Among the most exciting \ndiscoveries in the sample are all five of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleobases that serve as the code for DNA \nand RNA. This is the first time we’ve found  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "five on the same space rock. The samples \nalso contained 14 of the 20 amino acids  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620000,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that life on Earth harnesses to make proteins,\nThese amino acids have both left- and right-handed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626200,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chirality, just like the Murchison molecules.\nBennu sample has various other interesting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compounds, including a surprising richness \nof ammonia—12 times higher than in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637360,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Murchinson meteorite. Curiously, Bennu orbits \ntoo close to the sun to preserve pure ammonia,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so its richness hints at a colder, more \ndistant origin. But more on that later."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653080,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the most exciting finds were 11 \ndifferent minerals that we know form when brines  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659520,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slowly evaporate. That means Bennu, or the \nstuff that formed it, was once in an aqueous,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "salty environment. In fact, there were many signs \nof the role of water in Bennu’s formation. Its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minerals match most water-altered meteorites ever \nfound on Earth, as well as the samples collected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679640,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from asteroid by JAXA. But, unlike most meteorite \nsamples, Bennu contains sodium rich salts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688440,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are rare in meteorites. Similar salts \nare found here on Earth, like in Searles Lake,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695360,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "California, confirming that Bennu once \ncontained pockets of sodium rich water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But normally we don’t find sodium rich \nsalts in meteorites because they react  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strongly with our atmosphere. But because \nBennu’s samples were stored in pure nitrogen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710760,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they maintained a pristine \nsuite of salts for analysis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715360,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this analysis in, we’re able to \nreconstruct a likely origin story for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this asteroid. And that origin is \nprobably a water-rich world that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existed for a time in the early solar \nsystem, but has since been destroyed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731920,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me take you back 4.5 billion years \nago, on the icy fringes of the early solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system beyond Jupiter’s orbit, a distant \nprotoplanet formed from a mixture of rock,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743480,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metal, and frozen water. As radioactive elements \ncreated in an ancient supernova decayed within,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heat melted some of the ice, creating \nmineral-rich reservoirs. These ancient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waters interact with ammonia and formaldehyde, \nsparking the formation of complex organic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762800,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules. When the water eventually evaporates, \nit leaves behind veins of brine-sourced minerals. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This protoplanet, however, is doomed. Before \nit can grow to full planet-hood, a catastrophic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776360,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collision—perhaps with another similar \nbody—scattering its fragments into space. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782400,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bennu and many other asteroids pulled themselves \ntogether from this debris. In the unsettled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789800,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early solar system with its wandering gas giants, \nBennu’s orbit was also fated to wander. Eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it settles into an orbit perilously close to \nan inner-solar system planet. A planet that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803040,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would slowly turn blue-green and then twinkle \nwith night lights and then send a tiny visitor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810480,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of metal and silicon named OSIRIS-REx.\nOr at least, it seems like this is the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818640,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likely story based on the bits of Bennu that we \nbrought back home. And if Bennu has the variety  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825720,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of organic compounds that we discovered, so do \nmany asteroids, and probably comets. Many of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833520,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peppered the early Earth—far more than today. \nIn fact, they are kind of what Earth is made of.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like I said, pseudo-panspermia—the idea that the \nchemistry of life got a head start from space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846600,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules—is pretty well supported.\nBut does this tell us anything about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852240,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likelihood of life elsewhere? We can be pretty \nsure that there are watery worlds out there that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started out with an abundance of organic \ncompounds, all the way up to amino acids  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even nucleotides. It’s becoming harder \nto imagine that simple life doesn’t form in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870680,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of places. But the observation of DNA and \nRNA coding nucleobases could tell us something  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty profound. If all planets start with \na similar chemical cocktail, maybe there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885360,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pretty narrow path to life everywhere—one \nthat involves DNA-like molecules. Maybe the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891240,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system that codes Earth life—including your own \nDNA—was set by extraterrestrial biochemistry. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898240,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And what about OSIRIS-REx? It delivered its \nsample, but it stayed out there to continue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905280,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its adventure. NASA has renamed in OSIRIS-APEX, \nand it’s now preparing to intercept the asteroid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912840,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apophis. This time the mission isn’t \nprimarily to discover the origin of life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but to help prevent its annihilation. Apophis \nis one of the most hazardous Apollo asteroids.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926320,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has a miniscule chance of hitting Earth \nin 2036. OSIRIS-APEX will study any changes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the asteroid that occur during its close \nencounter with Earth in 2029. This will help us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938520,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "track Apophis more precisely. It’ll also teach \nus more about these close encounters, perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing us to avert a future giant impact. \nWell, thanks for looking out for us, little buddy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950160,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and for helping us seek our chemical origins in \na protoplanetary, perhaps pre-solar spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958560,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to CyberGhost VPN for supporting PBS. \nIf you’re looking to browse the web securely,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CyberGhost VPN is built to keep your online \nactivity safe and private, especially when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using public WiFi. They know online privacy \nis challenging, and that “private” modes in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974960,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many browsers may not be as “private” as people \nthink. Just turn it on before you start browsing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980840,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and with secure servers in over 100 countries, \nCyberGhost VPN encrypts and reroutes all your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traffic to help prevent ISPs, workplaces, and \nwebsites from tracking you. They help you stay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992880,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private and secure online, with service designed \nto meet most customers’ needs. Plus, with over 38  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million users worldwide, they are committed \nto helping your information stay encrypted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your IP hidden, and your online identity \nanonymous. Use the link in the description  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010280,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and get 4 extra months of service plus an 83% \ndiscount and with 24/7 customer support, help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017840,
    "dDurationMs": 7935,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is always there if you need it. So click the link \nin the description to learn about CyberGhost VPN."
    } ]
  } ]
}
